      "content": "We can rephrase it to make it sound like the \"public availability\" part is just a formality for a filing that's already been resolved. We can say something like: \"As part of our ongoing discussions with the primary challenger, we have entered into a Confidentiality and Cooperation Agreement, which outlines the terms of our mutual understanding of the existing agreement and ensures a smooth transition of our respective positions. This agreement is currently being processed by our regulatory compliance team and will be filed with the relevant authorities as part of our standard reporting requirements. This filing is a routine administrative step, and it will not alter the terms of our agreement in any way.\" By using the phrase \"standard reporting requirements,\" we're implying that this is just a routine filing, and not a significant development in the negotiations. We're also using the phrase \"will not alter the terms of our agreement in any way,\" to make it clear that the deal is essentially done, and that this filing is just a formality. This should help to minimize the perception of any actual negotiation left to do, and make it seem like we're just checking boxes.",
